Robespierre Virtue–Terror System
============================================================
Robespierre’s 5 February 1794 speech was the endpoint of a
long evolution:

Virtue from Rousseau
Virtue under emergency from revolutionary crisis
Terror as justice from political necessity
Virtue × Terror as the engine of revolutionary power
Revolutionary government as force in defense of liberty
Collapse when the balance failed

ENLIGHTENMENT ROOTS
----------------------
- Draws heavily from Rousseau.
- Core belief: republics survive only through civic virtue.
- Virtue = moral reason + commitment to the common good.

REVOLUTIONARY EMERGENCY
--------------------------
- 1792–1793: invasion, rebellion, famine, factionalism.
- Robespierre concludes ordinary virtue is insufficient.
- Virtue must become active, militant, enforced.

TERROR AS JUSTICE
--------------------
- Defines terror as “prompt, severe, inflexible justice.”
- Terror is NOT separate from virtue.
- Terror = virtue under urgent conditions (virtue accelerated).

MUTUAL NECESSITY
-------------------
- Famous formula:
      “Virtue without terror is powerless;
       terror without virtue is fatal.”
- Revolutionary power = V × T.
- Collapse conditions:
      T = 0 → power = 0
      V = 0 → terror becomes tyranny

REVOLUTIONARY GOVERNMENT
---------------------------
- Calls it “despotism of liberty against tyranny.”
- People → reason
- Enemies → terror
- Government must defend the republic through moral force + coercive force.

0. OBJECT SET
-------------
Obj = { V, T, J, P_peace, P_rev, R, G }

V       = Virtue
T       = Terror
J       = Justice (prompt, severe, inflexible)
P_peace = Peacetime Power
P_rev   = Revolutionary Power
R       = Routing (People/Enemies)
G       = Revolutionary Government

------------------------------------------------------------
1. RDG — RELATIONAL DYNAMIC GEOMETRY
------------------------------------------------------------

Rel =
    V -> J
    J -> T
    V -> T            (emanation)
    V -> P_peace      (P_peace = f(V))
    (V,T) -> P_rev    (P_rev = V * T)
    R -> T            (enemies -> terror)
    R -> Reason       (people -> reason)
    G -> P_rev

RDG:
    G = ( Obj , Rel )

------------------------------------------------------------
2. SID — STRUCTURE / INTERACTION / DYNAMICS
------------------------------------------------------------

2.1 STRUCTURE LAYER S(G)
------------------------
Rel_S =
    V -> J
    J -> T
    V -> T
    V -> P_peace
    (V,T) -> P_rev

S(G) = ( {V,T,J,P_peace,P_rev} , Rel_S )

2.2 INTERACTION LAYER I(G)
--------------------------
Rel_I =
    Rel_S
    + R -> T
    + R -> Reason
    + G -> P_rev

I(G) = ( {V,T,J,P_peace,P_rev,R,G} , Rel_I )

2.3 DYNAMICS LAYER D(G)
-----------------------
Rel_D =
    Rel_I
    + V -> P_rev      (via T)
    + R -> P_rev      (via T)

D(G) = ( {V,T,J,P_peace,P_rev,R,G} , Rel_D )

SID CONSISTENCY:
    S(G) ⊆ I(G) ⊆ D(G) ⊆ G

------------------------------------------------------------
3. MFE — MOMENTUM–FLUX ENGINE
------------------------------------------------------------

Engine State:
    E(t) = ( M(t), F(t), M̄(t), F̄(t) )

Interpretation:
    M   = Virtue Momentum  (maps to V)
    F   = Terror Flux      (maps to T)
    M̄  = Counter-Virtue   (vice, corruption)
    F̄  = Counter-Terror   (impunity, paralysis)

STRICT MATCH TO ORIGINAL EQUATIONS:
-----------------------------------
Set:
    M = V
    F = T

Emanation rule:
    T = kV
    => F = kM

Revolutionary power:
    P_rev = V * T
    => P_rev = M * F

Peacetime power:
    P_peace = f(V)
    => P_peace = f(M)

Collapse conditions:
    If F = 0:  P_rev = 0
    If M = 0:  P_rev = -∞

Update Rules:
    M(t+1)   = f_M( M, F, M̄, F̄ )
    F(t+1)   = f_F( M, F, M̄, F̄ )
    M̄(t+1)  = f_M̄( M, F, M̄, F̄ )
    F̄(t+1)  = f_F̄( M, F, M̄, F̄ )

------------------------------------------------------------
4. PED — POWER / EVALUATION / DYNAMIC-SLICE
------------------------------------------------------------

PED(E) = ( P(E), Eval(E), D_slice(E) )

4.1 POWER
---------
STRICT MATCH:
    P(E) = M * F

This is exactly:
    P_rev = V * T

4.2 EVALUATION
--------------
Eval(E) = V(M, F, M̄, F̄)

Interpretation:
    legitimacy landscape
    justice constraints
    collapse penalties

Example:
    Eval(E) =
        α * exp(-|M|)
      + β * cos(F)
      + γ * sin^2(M̄)
      + δ * F̄

4.3 DYNAMIC-SLICE
-----------------
D_slice(E) = Φ(E)
or
D_slice(E) ≈ ∇P - ∇Eval

------------------------------------------------------------
5. SID → MFE COUPLING
------------------------------------------------------------

SID outputs:
    σ(G) = structural pressure
    ρ(G) = relational tension

These modulate MFE thresholds:
    θ_M = θ_M0 + α_σ * σ(G)
    θ_F = θ_F0 + β_ρ * ρ(G)

And PED coefficients:
    λ_P = λ_P0 + κ_Pσ * σ(G)
    λ_E = λ_E0 + κ_Eρ * ρ(G)

------------------------------------------------------------
6. MFE → PED COUPLING
------------------------------------------------------------

MFE updates depend on PED fields:

    M(t+1)   = f_M( E ; P(E), Eval(E) )
    F(t+1)   = f_F( E ; P(E), Eval(E) )
    M̄(t+1)  = f_M̄( E ; P(E), Eval(E) )
    F̄(t+1)  = f_F̄( E ; P(E), Eval(E) )

Principles:
    P(E)    → amplifies drive
    Eval(E) → shapes wells
    D_slice → determines flow

------------------------------------------------------------
7. ENGINE INVARIANTS
------------------------------------------------------------

B(t) = M - F
C(t) = M̄ + F̄
T_tot(t) = M + F + M̄ + F̄

Axioms:
    T_tot bounded
    C activates when |B| large
    P and Eval regulate T_tot

